@slim: 1.0
@+title: SLIM — Structured LLM Instruction Markup
@+version: 1.0.0
@+status: draft
@+authors: Sasidhar Nagandla
@+date: 2026-05-10
@+license: TBD (open source)
@+extension: .slm
@+mime: text/slim
@+encoding: UTF-8

~ ============================================================
~ SLIM v1.0 — FORMAL SPECIFICATION
~ ============================================================

# 1. Overview

SLIM (Structured LLM Instruction Markup) is a plain-text file format designed for AI
documentation, agent configuration, and prompt engineering. It replaces Markdown as the
primary format for files consumed by Large Language Models (LLMs) and AI orchestration
systems.

SLIM achieves an average 43% token reduction over Markdown on real-world AI agent files
(measured on cl100k_base tokenizer, 6-file benchmark, 2026-05-10).

## 1.1 Design Goals
- Token efficiency: minimize characters sent to LLM without losing semantic precision
- Semantic clarity: every structural element has unambiguous, single meaning
- Dual-author: readable and writable by both humans and AI systems
- Injection safety: hard block boundaries prevent prompt injection attacks
- All-purpose: covers system prompts, agent configs, tool schemas, docs, orchestration
- Parser-friendly: unambiguous grammar, no context-sensitive edge cases

## 1.2 Core Architecture — Three Zones

Every SLIM file is divided into three strictly ordered zones:

=== ZONE_DIAGRAM [ascii]
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  HEADER ZONE  (@key: value lines)                   │
│  Consumer: Orchestrator/parser only                 │
│  LLM sees: NO (stripped before LLM call)            │
│  Exception: @+ prefixed lines → LLM sees YES        │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  BODY ZONE  (prose, headings, bullets, directives)  │
│  Consumer: LLM (primary) + parser (variables)       │
│  LLM sees: YES (after variable interpolation)       │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  BLOCK ZONE  (=== NAME ... === /NAME)               │
│  Consumer: LLM sees block content verbatim          │
│  Parser: extracts blocks as named typed payloads    │
│  Location: anywhere in Body Zone                    │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
=== /ZONE_DIAGRAM

# 2. Header Zone

## 2.1 Syntax

The header zone begins at the first line of the file and ends at the first line that is
neither an @-prefixed line, a blank line, nor a --- separator.

```
@key: value
@+key: value
@include: path
```

Rules:
- Key must be lowercase ASCII: [a-z][a-z0-9_-]*
- Separator: first ": " (colon + space). Value may contain subsequent colons.
- Multi-line value: continuation with 2-space indent on next line(s)
- Header zone terminates at first # heading or non-blank non-@ line

## 2.2 Strip Semantics

| Prefix | LLM sees? | Variable interpolated? | Notes |
| @key   | No        | Yes ($key → value)     | Orchestrator-only metadata |
| @+key  | Yes       | Yes ($key → value)     | Context the LLM needs |

This is the primary token efficiency mechanism. Configuration metadata never reaches LLM.

## 2.3 Reserved Header Keys

| Key         | Type     | Description |
| @slim       | str      | Format version declaration. Required. |
| @agent      | str      | Agent identifier (use @+agent to send to LLM) |
| @model      | str      | Target model. Never sent to LLM (@model only). |
| @mode       | str      | Execution mode |
| @retry      | int      | Retry limit for orchestrator |
| @timeout    | str      | Timeout duration (e.g. 30s, 5m) |
| @tags       | list<str>| Metadata tags for indexing |
| @include    | url/path | Import another .slm file (resolved first) |
| @version    | str      | Document version |

Custom keys are permitted. All keys parsed as typed values (see Section 6).

## 2.4 Type Coercion

Header values are automatically typed:

| Value Pattern | Inferred Type | Example |
| true / false  | bool          | @async: true |
| Integer       | int           | @retry: 3 |
| Decimal       | float         | @threshold: 0.7 |
| null / none   | null          | @context: null |
| a, b, c       | list<str>     | @stack: python, js, go |
| "42"          | str (forced)  | @id: "42" |
| Anything else | str           | @name: SecurityBot |

## 2.5 @include Directive

```
@include: ./base/agent.slm
@include: ./tools/mcp_tools.slm
```

- Resolved by parser before any other processing
- Included file's @+ headers merge into current file's LLM context
- Included file's @ headers are defaults (current file overrides)
- Circular includes: parse error
- Max include depth: 5 levels
- @include is always stripped (@+include is invalid)

## 2.6 Multi-Document Files

Use --- (triple dash on its own line) to separate independent documents in one file.
Each document is parsed independently with its own header zone.

```
@slim: 1.0
@+agent: Planner
# Role
You plan tasks.
---
@slim: 1.0
@+agent: Executor
# Role
You execute tasks.
```

# 3. Body Zone

## 3.1 Structural Elements

| Sigil   | Meaning            | Scope |
| #       | H1 section heading | Body  |
| ##      | H2 subsection      | Body  |
| ###     | H3 deep section    | Body  |
| -       | Bullet point       | Body  |
| 1.      | Ordered step       | Body  |
| >KEYWORD| Directive command  | Body  |
| $key    | Variable reference | Body  |
| \|col\| | Table row          | Body  |
| ~       | Comment (stripped) | Anywhere |
| \X      | Literal escape     | Anywhere |

## 3.2 Directives

Directives are lines beginning with > followed immediately by a reserved ALL_CAPS keyword.
No space between > and keyword is required; one space is canonical.

Reserved keywords: CALL, ASSERT, YIELD, EMIT, LOG, ABORT, WAIT, RETRY

```
> CALL tool_name(param: value, param2: $var)
> ASSERT $result.status == complete
> YIELD $result.report
> EMIT event_name(payload: $data)
> LOG "Step completed"
> ABORT "Reason for stopping"
> WAIT 5s
> RETRY 3
```

A > followed by anything other than a reserved keyword is treated as literal text.
Example: "> 90% of bugs..." — not a directive.

## 3.3 Variable References

$key references are interpolated by the parser before the body is sent to the LLM.

- $key — simple reference (must match a header key, case-sensitive)
- $result.field — runtime dotpath reference (resolved by orchestrator at runtime)
- $list[0] — indexed reference (runtime)
- Literal dollar sign: \$amount → "$amount" verbatim
- Variables in content containing newlines: only first line used

## 3.4 Tables

Table rows use pipe-delimited syntax. No separator row is required (unlike Markdown).
First row is treated as the header.

```
| Format | Tokens | Readable |
| JSON   | 340    | Medium   |
| SLIM   | 175    | High     |
```

Empty cells: || (adjacent pipes). Alignment: parser does not enforce, renderers may.

## 3.5 Inline Formatting

Markdown-compatible inline formatting (optional, parser passes through verbatim):

- `code` — inline code
- *bold* — bold text
- _italic_ — italic text

No links in v1. No inline images (use === BLOCK [base64:type] for images).

## 3.6 Comments

Lines starting with ~ (tilde) are stripped by all SLIM parsers before any output.
They never reach the LLM. Use for author notes, TODOs, disabled content.

```
~ Author: Jane Doe
~ TODO: add edge case for empty input
~ @+agent: DisabledAgent  ← this line is never parsed
```

# 4. Block Zone

## 4.1 Syntax

```
=== BLOCK_NAME
content
=== /BLOCK_NAME

=== BLOCK_NAME [type]
typed content
=== /BLOCK_NAME
```

Rules:
- Block names: SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE, ASCII only, [A-Z][A-Z0-9_]*
- Closing tag must exactly mirror the opening name (case-insensitive match)
- Type tag is optional: [python], [json], [sql], [bash], [raw], [csv], [base64:png], etc.
- Blocks can appear anywhere in the body zone
- Nesting: single level only (one block inside another is valid; deeper is a parse error)
- Empty blocks are valid (parser returns empty string for content)

## 4.2 Block Content Rules

- Content is captured verbatim (whitespace preserved, blank lines preserved)
- Escape: any line inside a block starting with === must be prefixed with \
  Example: \=== /MY_BLOCK → literal "=== /MY_BLOCK" in content
- Parsers must reject unescaped === lines inside block content at parse time

## 4.3 Nested Blocks (single level)

Useful for few-shot examples and grouped content sets:

```
=== EXAMPLES

=== EXAMPLE_1
user: Is this SQL safe?
assistant: No — SQL injection risk on line 2.
=== /EXAMPLE_1

=== EXAMPLE_2
user: How do I fix it?
assistant: Use parameterized queries.
=== /EXAMPLE_2

=== /EXAMPLES
```

## 4.4 Special Block Types

| Type         | Meaning |
| [python]     | Python source code |
| [js]         | JavaScript source |
| [json]       | JSON data |
| [sql]        | SQL query |
| [bash]       | Shell commands |
| [raw]        | Uninterpreted text |
| [csv]        | Comma-separated data |
| [ascii]      | ASCII art / diagrams |
| [base64:png] | Base64-encoded PNG image |
| [base64:jpg] | Base64-encoded JPEG image |

# 5. Schema Definitions

## 5.1 Tool Schema Syntax

Tool schemas define callable functions/tools available to the agent.
Schema blocks begin with :tool_name (colon prefix) and use 2-space indented properties.

```
:tool_name
  desc: Human-readable description of what this tool does
  param1!: type
  param2?: type = default_value
  param3?: [option_a|option_b|option_c] = option_a
  -> return_field: type, return_field2: type
```

Property suffixes:
- ! = required (parse error if missing at call time)
- ? = optional
- = value = default value (implies optional)

## 5.2 Custom Type Definitions

```
=== TYPE_DEF
  TypeName:
    field1!: type
    field2?: type = default
=== /TYPE_DEF
```

Custom types can then be used in schema definitions: `-> results: list<TypeName>`

## 5.3 Complete Schema Example

```
=== TYPE_DEF
  Finding:
    line!: int
    severity!: [low|medium|high|critical]
    message!: str
    fix?: str
=== /TYPE_DEF

:analyze_code
  desc: Run static analysis on a code snippet
  code!: str
  language?: [python|js|java|go|rust] = python
  ruleset?: [owasp|cwe|custom] = owasp
  depth?: int = 3
  -> score: float, findings: list<Finding>, summary: str
```

# 6. Type System

## 6.1 Primitive Types

| Type     | Description | Examples |
| str      | UTF-8 string | "hello", hello |
| int      | Integer | 3, -10, 0 |
| float    | Decimal | 0.7, -1.5, 3.14 |
| bool     | Boolean | true, false |
| null     | Null/None | null, none |

## 6.2 First-Class Types

| Type     | Description | Validation |
| datetime | ISO 8601 datetime | 2026-05-10T14:30:00Z |
| url      | Valid URL | https://example.com |
| uuid     | UUID v4 | 550e8400-e29b-41d4-a716 |
| json     | Arbitrary JSON object | Any valid JSON |

## 6.3 Collection Types

| Type       | Syntax | Example |
| Enum       | [a\|b\|c] | [prod\|staging\|dev] |
| List       | list<T> | list<str>, list<Finding> |
| Dict       | dict<K,V> | dict<str,int> |

## 6.4 User-Defined Types

Defined in === TYPE_DEF blocks. Reusable across schema definitions in the same file
or in files that @include this file.

# 7. Security Model

## 7.1 Zone Ordering Enforcement

The header zone MUST precede the body zone. Any @key found after the first # heading
or non-blank non-@ line is treated as literal body text, not a header.

This prevents header injection: user-provided content placed before headers cannot
inject @model, @include, or other orchestrator-controlled fields.

## 7.2 Block Boundary Injection

All parsers MUST validate that block content does not contain unescaped === lines.
Unescaped === inside a block = parse error (strict mode) or warning (lenient mode).

## 7.3 Variable Value Sanitization

Variable values containing newline characters: only first line is used.
Parsers MUST provide sanitize_user_content(str) → str that escapes:
  @, $, >, ===, ~  →  \@, \$, \>, \===, \~

Use this function on any user-provided string before embedding in a SLIM file.

## 7.4 @include Path Traversal

@include paths are resolved relative to the including file's directory.
Absolute paths: allowed but should be flagged by linters.
Network URLs: parser-implementation-dependent (disabled by default).
Circular includes: detect and raise parse error.

# 8. Parser Behaviour

## 8.1 Parse Modes

| Mode    | On error | Use case |
| strict  | Raise exception with line number | Linters, CI validation |
| lenient | Collect warnings, return partial result | LLM-facing production parsers |

## 8.2 Output Structure

A parsed SLIM document produces:

```
SLIMDocument:
  version: str
  headers: dict<str, any>          # all @ headers (stripped)
  llm_headers: dict<str, any>      # @+ headers (LLM-visible)
  sections: list<Section>          # # heading + content
  blocks: dict<str, Block>         # named blocks by name
  schemas: dict<str, Schema>       # :tool definitions
  directives: list<Directive>      # > KEYWORD calls
  body_text: str                   # full body, variables interpolated
  stripped_text: str               # body_text minus ~ comments
```

## 8.3 Strip Mode

strip_mode=True removes @ headers and ~ comments before returning body_text.
This is the text that should be sent to the LLM.

## 8.4 Round-Trip Canonical Form

slim fmt produces canonical form:
- One blank line between sections
- No trailing whitespace
- Block names in SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE
- Schema properties: required (!) first, optional (?) second, alphabetical within each group
- Consistent 2-space indentation in schemas

## 8.5 Whitespace Rules

| Rule |
| Indentation: spaces only. Tabs = parse error in schemas. |
| Blank lines between sections: ignored. |
| Blank lines inside === blocks: preserved verbatim. |
| Trailing spaces on all lines: stripped. |
| BOM (UTF-8 byte order mark): stripped silently. |

# 9. BNF Grammar

```
document    ::= header_zone body_zone
header_zone ::= (header_line | blank_line)*
header_line ::= at_key ": " value newline
              | "@include" ": " path newline
at_key      ::= ("@" | "@+") [a-z][a-z0-9_-]*
body_zone   ::= (section | block | schema | directive | table_row
                | bullet | ordered_step | paragraph | comment | blank_line)*
section     ::= "#"+ " " text newline
block       ::= block_open block_content block_close
block_open  ::= "===" " " BLOCK_NAME (" [" type "]")? newline
block_close ::= "===" " /" BLOCK_NAME newline
block_content ::= (escaped_line | non_boundary_line)*
escaped_line  ::= "\===" text newline
schema      ::= ":" identifier newline property+
property    ::= "  " identifier ("!" | "?")? ": " type (" = " value)? newline
              | "  " "->" " " return_spec newline
directive   ::= ">" " " KEYWORD (" " args)? newline
table_row   ::= "|" (cell "|")+ newline
bullet      ::= "-" " " text newline
ordered_step ::= [0-9]+ "." " " text newline
comment     ::= "~" text? newline
variable_ref ::= "$" identifier ("." identifier | "[" integer "]")*
BLOCK_NAME  ::= [A-Z][A-Z0-9_]*
KEYWORD     ::= "CALL" | "ASSERT" | "YIELD" | "EMIT" | "LOG" | "ABORT" | "WAIT" | "RETRY"
```

# 10. File Metadata

| Property  | Value |
| Extension | .slm |
| MIME type | text/slim |
| Encoding  | UTF-8 (BOM optional, stripped) |
| Line endings | LF preferred, CRLF tolerated |
| Max header value | 2048 characters |
| Max include depth | 5 levels |
| Max block nesting | 1 level |
| Block name charset | ASCII uppercase + underscore + digits |

# 11. Migration Guide from Markdown

## 11.1 Structural Equivalences

| Markdown | SLIM | Notes |
| --- frontmatter | @key: value header | SLIM strips by default; use @+ to expose |
| # Heading | # Heading | Identical |
| - bullet | - bullet | Identical |
| 1. step | 1. step | Identical |
| \`\`\`lang code \`\`\` | === BLOCK [lang] ... === /BLOCK | Named + typed |
| <!-- comment --> | ~ comment | Shorter |
| \| col \| col \| \n \|---\|---\| | \| col \| col \| (no separator row) | Saves ~6 tokens per table |
| *bold* | *bold* | Identical |
| \`inline\` | \`inline\` | Identical |

## 11.2 SLIM-Only Features (no Markdown equivalent)

- @key header stripping (tokens never reach LLM)
- $variable interpolation
- > DIRECTIVE command syntax
- :tool schema definitions with typed params
- === block type tags
- ~ comments (stripped silently, not passed to LLM)
- === boundary injection protection
- list<T>, dict<K,V>, [enum|values] types

## 11.3 Migration Steps

1. Rename file from .md to .slm
2. Convert YAML frontmatter (---) to @key: / @+key: headers
3. Remove --- frontmatter delimiters
4. Replace ``` code blocks with === BLOCK_NAME [lang] ... === /BLOCK_NAME
5. Replace <!-- comments --> with ~ comments
6. Remove | --- | separator rows from all tables
7. Add @slim: 1.0 as first header line
8. Run: slim validate yourfile.slm

# 12. Versioning

SLIM version is declared in @slim: header. Current: 1.0

| Version | Status | Notes |
| 1.0     | Current | Base spec |
| 1.0+    | Planned | + tables, imports, multi-doc, nested blocks |
| 1.0s    | Current | + schema definitions (:tool, TYPE_DEF) |
| 2.0     | Future  | + ? conditional blocks, loop constructs |

# 13. Complete Reference Example

=== FULL_EXAMPLE [slim]
@slim: 1.0s
@include: ./base/code_reviewer.slm
@model: claude-opus-4
@retry: 3
@timeout: 60s
@+agent: SecurityBot
@+task: PR-942
@+env: production

~ Security review flow for incoming pull requests

# Role
You are $agent performing a security audit on $task in $env.

# Rules
- Score risk 0.0-1.0. Above 0.7 blocks the PR automatically.
- Cite exact line number for each finding.
- Use [CRITICAL] / [HIGH] / [MEDIUM] / [LOW] labels inline.

# Steps
1. Read USER_CODE block below
2. > CALL analyze_risk(pr: $task, code: $input, env: $env)
3. > ASSERT $result.score != null
4. > YIELD $result.report

=== USER_CODE [python]
def get_user(id):
    query = f"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id={id}"
    return db.execute(query)
=== /USER_CODE

=== EXAMPLES
=== EXAMPLE_1
user: Is f"SELECT * WHERE id={id}" safe?
assistant: No. [CRITICAL] Line 2 — SQL injection. Use: db.execute("SELECT * WHERE id=?", (id,))
=== /EXAMPLE_1
=== /EXAMPLES

:analyze_risk
  desc: Score a PR for security vulnerabilities
  pr!: str
  code!: str
  env!: [prod|staging|dev]
  ruleset?: [owasp|cwe|custom] = owasp
  depth?: int = 3
  -> score: float, findings: list<Finding>, summary: str

=== TYPE_DEF
  Finding:
    line!: int
    severity!: [low|medium|high|critical]
    message!: str
    fix?: str
=== /TYPE_DEF
=== /FULL_EXAMPLE
